{"result": {"response": {"id": "resp_0b7e03b0f40585fa0169bb40d9da448191a6932052fd4d8b5e", "created": 1773879513, "model": "gpt-5.2-2025-12-11", "object": "chat.completion", "system_fingerprint": null, "choices": [{"finish_reason": "stop", "index": 0, "message": {"content": "{\"title\":\"Regional Lymph Nodes, Nodal Metastasis, and Breast Cancer Staging Anatomy\",\"summary\":\"This community centers on the clinical concept of REGIONAL LYMPH NODES and how their involvement is defined as LYMPH NODE METASTASIS, which is used in cancer staging and risk stratification. The network further specifies key regional nodal groups relevant to breast cancer staging—AXILLARY (underarm) nodes, INTERNAL MAMMARY nodes (near the breastbone), and INFRACLAVICULAR/SUPRACLAVICULAR nodes (around the collarbone). A related imaging concept (MRI WITH CONTRAST) is connected via an anatomic field-of-view reference (from the COLLARBONE to the skull base), linking surveillance imaging description to the same anatomic landmark used in nodal region delineation.\",\"findings\":[{\"summary\":\"Regional lymph node involvement is explicitly defined as lymph node metastasis\",\"explanation\":\"The central definitional relationship in this community is that cancer found in a regional lymph node is explicitly defined as a lymph node metastasis. This creates a direct conceptual bridge between staging anatomy (regional nodes) and disease spread terminology (metastasis). [Data: Entities (1697, 1698); Relationships (2779)]\\n\\nREGIONAL LYMPH NODES are described as lymph nodes located near the primary tumor and often the first site involved when cancer spreads, and their involvement is treated as “regional spread” affecting TNM staging (N category). LYMPH NODE METASTASIS is described as the presence of cancer cells in regional nodes indicating spread beyond the original site; together with the explicit relationship, the community frames nodal assessment as a core determinant of staging and downstream clinical 