d as an anatomic landmark used to define the lower boundary of a recommended MRI imaging field (extending from the collarbone to the skull base). Separately, it is described as relevant for assessing infraclavicular and supraclavicular lymph node involvement and for delineating treatment areas. [Data: Entities (3885, 2767); Relationships (9613)]\\n\\nThe relationships also tie the collarbone directly to INFRACLAVICULAR NODES by describing them as being near the collarbone region, supporting the idea that the same landmark underpins both imaging protocols (field-of-view) and the spatial organization of nodal staging regions. [Data: Entities (3885, 3891); Relationships (8132, 9613)]\"},{\"summary\":\"Breastbone anchors the internal mammary nodal region in the staging anatomy network\",\"explanation\":\"BREASTBONE is included as an anatomic landmark near which lymph nodes may be involved in breast cancer spread, specifically referencing internal mammary nodes. This provides the structural context for where internal mammary nodes are located and why they are considered in staging. [Data: Entities (3886, 3890)]\\n\\nA direct relationship further links BREASTBONE to INTERNAL MAMMARY NODES by stating that internal mammary nodes are described as being near the breastbone, reinforcing this anatomic association as part of the community’s staging geography. [Data: Relationships (8134); Entities (3886, 3890)]\"},{\"summary\":\"The community emphasizes staging and clinical decision implications tied to nodal assessment\",\"explanation\":\"REGIONAL LYMPH NODES are described as a key factor in cancer staging (specifically the TNM N category), prognosis, and treatment planning, and are routinely assessed and sometimes surgically removed for examination or targeted with local therapies. This positions nodal assessment not as a peripheral detail, but as a core driver of clinical management. [Data: Entities (1697)]\\n\\nLYMPH NODE METASTASIS is described (in the context of thyroid cancer) as a key factor for disease stage and overall risk stratification, with the extent of nodal involvement (e.g., number of affected nodes, size of metastatic deposits) influencing decisions such as whether to recommend radioactive iodine therapy and how aggressively to monitor disease. This complements the breast-cancer-focused anatomy by showing nodal status as broadly consequential across cancers. [Data: Entities (1698)]\"},{\"summary\":\"MRI with contrast is linked into the network through an anatomic imaging range rather than direct nodal evaluation\",\"explanation\":\"MRI WITH CONTRAST is described as a contrast-enhanced pelvic MRI used in surveillance imaging schedules following rectal cancer endoscopic/surgical treatment, indicating it belongs to an imaging/protocol subcomponent rather than the breast nodal staging subcomponent. [Data: Entities (2767)]\\n\\nIts explicit relationship to COLLARBONE specifies that the MRI with contrast is described as extending from the collarbone to the skull base. This connection integrates imaging protocol language with the same anatomic landmark used to describe nodal basins (infraclavicular/supraclavicular) in the rest of the community, even though no direct relationship is provided between the MRI and specific lymph node groups. [Data: Relationships (9613); Entities (2767, 3885)]\"}],\"rating\":2.6,\"rating_explanation\":\"Impact is low-to-moderate because the community is primarily clinical/anatomical knowledge about lymph node staging and imaging landmarks, without evidence of harmful intent or operational capability.\"}", "role": "assistant", "tool_calls": null, "function_call": null}, "provider_specific_fields": {"native_finish_reason": "stop"}}], "usage": {"completion_tokens": 1550, "prompt_tokens": 3141, "total_tokens": 4691, "completion_tokens_details": {"accepted_prediction_tokens": null, "audio_tokens": null, "reasoning_tokens": 0, "rejected_prediction_tokens": null, "text_tokens": null}, "prompt_tokens_details